ht and we think about all  the various contingencies that are  likely to crop up and all the  complexities if we communicate early and  often with our parent  about what those plans are and if we are  patient with each other about the need  for calling audibles along the way I  think our kids can be back in school in  some shape form or fashion and those are  gonna be hybrids sometimes in person and  sometimes remotely you know the  president calls the CDC guidelines he  calls them tough and expensive but when  it comes to children's health is there  any thing as being too tough or too  expensive there are still so many  unknowns as you know Madam Secretary  with this coronavirus well this is all  about risk management and and as has  been said and I agree with completely  there are downsides to having our  students out of school for so long  learning loss is real for students of  all kinds the functioning of our economy  and of families the ability for parents  to work I mean there are major downsides  to not having our students actively  engaged in their own learning and that's  what we need to balance but yes I mean  the cares Act had resources available my  own state of Texas is using those  resources to provide PPE and and various  other things cleaning materials and  supplies to schools so that they can  manage that risk as well as possible and  of course insisting on masks to the  maximum extent so it's a balancing act  will it be perfect it will not and  parents will need to be active  participants in the decision-making in  this regard but you've got the president  who to most people's knowledge doesn't  appear to have any medical training and  now he's slamming his own experts who  have the medical training and who are  looking at the science does that concern  you a little bit you know  what I'm seeing is that is that  educators County judges and County  health officials governor's are really  not looking to Washington for guidance  on this matter they know that every  single community is different their  health issues are different every single  person's a student and teacher their  issues are different and so this is  absolutely a you know locally driven  hurt most of the money for our schools  comes from state and local governments  the federal government is a minor  investor certainly can use a bully  pulpit to advance best practices and  provide clear communication but I think  parents are looking to their local  officials to understand the needs of  that community and that balancing act  and be a party to those decisions so you  think you're saying it's best to leave  it up to the state then to make their  own individual decisions the way it's  set up to do that's right  and they're the people who are paying  the bills and so honestly there's no way  for the present to withhold fundings  that have been a funding that's been  appropriated by Congress for particular  things that could they do that going  forward in a new piece of legislation  yes potentially yeah to add that  dimension to a really stressful  situation that's extremely complex is  just one more thing we shouldn't be  worrying about right now we need to have  certainty as much as possible around  funding around contingency planning and  around the health and safety and  educational needs of our students and  families so right now the guidelines  call for this face covering social  distancing staggered scheduling no  communal space if you were advising the  president and advising schools what do  you think would be the best course of  action here and parents who are  concerned saying I'm not sending my kids  back to school right now well I think  parents need options and I think  ultimately these are parental decisions  and if a parent elects not to send their  child into a situation where yes there's  more social distancing and the school  has done everything they can provided  masks not have the cafeteria open all  those things but if a parent is